# Files whose line endings are not a matter of taste.
#
# The bash integration script is sourced by bash itself, on the other side of a
# Git Bash or WSL boundary. A carriage return is syntax to bash, not whitespace:
# with CRLF endings it does not merely look untidy, it fails to parse —
#
#   bash: $'\r': command not found
#   integration.bash: line 19: syntax error near unexpected token `$'{\r''
#
# — and every shell that sources it comes up with no integration at all. Git is
# storing the file with LF; it was `core.autocrlf` rewriting it on checkout, so a
# clone on a Windows machine with the usual settings shipped a broken script while
# the committed one was fine. Nothing in the repo said otherwise until now.
*.bash text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
